WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.260
Welcome to ValiStera Academy. In this

2
00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:05.720
lesson, we will explore the fundamentals of

3
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.640
good manufacturing practice, commonly known as GMP.

4
00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.250
Pharmaceutical manufacturing requires more than producing a

5
00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.750
medicine that passes laboratory testing. It requires

6
00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:22.190
controlled processes, trained personnel, suitable equipment, and

7
00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:23.390
reliable documentation.

8
00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:26.070
By the end of this lesson, you

9
00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.290
will understand the purpose of GMP, its

10
00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:31.750
core principles and how it contributes to

11
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.290
patient safety and consistent product quality.

12
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.170
Every pharmaceutical product is intended to deliver

13
00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:42.270
a specific therapeutic benefit. However, failures in

14
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:45.210
manufacturing controls can create serious risks.

15
00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:49.870
These risks may include contamination, incorrect strength,

16
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:54.290
product mix-ups, or inconsistent performance. Many

17
00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.690
manufacturing defects cannot be identified simply by

18
00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.670
testing a small sample of the finished

19
00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:00.070
batch.

20
00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.560
That is why pharmaceutical quality must be

21
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:08.300
systematically managed throughout manufacturing, testing, packaging and

22
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.540
storage. GMP provides essential controls to reduce

23
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.460
these risks and protect patients.

24
00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.840
GMP stands for Good Manufacturing Practice. It

25
00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.200
is a system of principles and controls

26
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.640
intended to ensure that pharmaceutical products are

27
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.600
consistently manufactured and controlled according to appropriate

28
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:30.560
quality standards.

29
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:35.070
GMP covers activities such as personnel training,

30
00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:40.570
equipment maintenance, cleaning, manufacturing operations, quality control

31
00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:44.550
and documentation. For example, a pharmaceutical mixing

32
00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.510
vessel should be suitable for its intended

33
00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:50.310
purpose, properly maintained and cleaned according to

34
00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.130
approved procedures.

35
00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.250
GMP is not a single test or

36
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:58.010
certificate. It is an ongoing system of

37
00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:00.390
controlled operations and quality oversight.

38
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:05.170
A pharmaceutical quality system, or PQS, brings

39
00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:07.610
together the arrangements used to manage product

40
00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.970
quality. GMP is an essential part of

41
00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:11.430
this system.

42
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.510
Other important elements include quality risk management,

43
00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:20.170
change management, corrective and preventive actions, management

44
00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:24.570
responsibilities, and continual improvement. For example, if

45
00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:27.550
a recurring equipment problem is identified, the

46
00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:31.530
quality system should support investigation, appropriate corrective

47
00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:34.250
action, and evaluation of whether the action

48
00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:34.910
was effective.

49
00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:38.930
An effective quality system is therefore proactive

50
00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:40.090
as well as reactive.

51
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.920
One of the most important GMP principles

52
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.340
is that finished product testing alone cannot

53
00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.040
provide complete assurance of quality. Consider a

54
00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.060
manufacturing process with inadequate mixing controls.

55
00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.170
Even if the finished product samples meet

56
00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:00.550
their specifications, that result alone may not

57
00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.430
demonstrate that every part of the batch

58
00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:07.910
was manufactured consistently. GMP, therefore, emphasizes suitable

59
00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.930
process design, defined operating parameters, in-process

60
00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.070
controls, monitoring and documented verification.

61
00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.970
Testing remains essential, but it works together

62
00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:22.190
with manufacturing controls rather than replacing them.

63
00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:25.410
People play a critical role in pharmaceutical

64
00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:30.050
quality. Personnel must have appropriate qualifications, training

65
00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:32.750
and experience for their assigned responsibilities.

66
00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:37.970
Training should cover relevant GMP principles, approved

67
00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:42.530
procedures, and specific operational activities. For example,

68
00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.890
an operator responsible for equipment cleaning should

69
00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:49.490
understand the approved cleaning procedure, required precautions,

70
00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.430
and associated documentation.

71
00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:56.190
Training effectiveness and continuing competence should be

72
00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:59.390
managed according to applicable requirements and company

73
00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:02.910
procedures. Having a training record does not

74
00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:05.530
automatically demonstrate that a person can perform

75
00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:07.470
every assigned activity correctly.

76
00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:13.510
Pharmaceutical manufacturing requires suitable premises, equipment and

77
00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:17.130
supporting utilities. Facilities should be designed and

78
00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.470
maintained to support controlled operations and reduce

79
00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.550
contamination or mix -up risks.

80
00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.730
Equipment should be appropriate for its intended

81
00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:29.250
use and subject to relevant maintenance, calibration

82
00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.970
and qualification activities. Supporting utilities, such as

83
00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:38.170
pharmaceutical water, compressed gases, and HVAC systems,

84
00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.530
may also require defined controls depending on

85
00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:43.830
their intended use and associated risks.

86
00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:49.170
Equipment qualification helps provide documented evidence that

87
00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.390
a system is suitable for its intended

88
00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:54.790
purpose. We will explore qualification in detail

89
00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:55.790
in later lessons.

90
00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.780
Documentation is fundamental to GMP because it

91
00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.340
communicates instructions and provides evidence of performed

92
00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:09.140
activities. Standard operating procedures describe how defined

93
00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:10.860
activities should be carried out.

94
00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:15.130
Records demonstrate what was actually performed, observed,

95
00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:19.250
and reviewed. GMP documentation should be accurate,

96
00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:23.930
attributable, legible, contemporaneous, and appropriately retained.

97
00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:27.870
For example, when an equipment qualification test

98
00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.450
is executed, the results should be recorded

99
00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:33.270
in accordance with the approved protocol and

100
00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:38.710
applicable documentation procedures. Reliable records support traceability,

101
00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:41.070
investigations, and quality decisions.

102
00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:46.610
Manufacturing and quality control have complementary responsibilities.

103
00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:51.250
Manufacturing personnel carry out production activities according

104
00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:54.570
to approved instructions and defined process controls.

105
00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:59.270
Quality control performs sampling, testing, and other

106
00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:02.970
laboratory-related activities according to established procedures.

107
00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:07.490
The pharmaceutical quality system also includes independent

108
00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:10.930
quality oversight and defined responsibilities for reviewing

109
00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:15.350
records, evaluating deviations and authorizing quality decisions.

110
00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.120
A batch should not be released merely

111
00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:21.780
because a laboratory result is acceptable. The

112
00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:26.300
applicable manufacturing, documentation and release requirements must

113
00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:27.240
also be satisfied.

114
00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:32.420
Even well-controlled pharmaceutical operations can experience

115
00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:36.060
unexpected events. A deviation occurs when an

116
00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.780
activity or result differs from an applicable

117
00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:41.780
approved requirement or established expectation.

118
00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:46.200
Deviations should be documented and evaluated according

119
00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.780
to their potential impact. Where appropriate, an

120
00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:54.240
investigation identifies contributing factors and supports corrective

121
00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:55.660
or preventive actions.

122
00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:59.520
Change control provides a structured approach for

123
00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.060
evaluating proposed changes before implementation, including their

124
00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.980
potential effect on product quality and validated

125
00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:11.480
status. These systems help organizations maintain control

126
00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:13.120
and improve their processes.

127
00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.440
Consider a pharmaceutical manufacturing vessel prepared for

128
00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:20.900
production. Before use, personnel may need to

129
00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:25.280
verify the equipment's identification, cleaning status, calibration

130
00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:28.280
status of relevant instruments and readiness for

131
00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:32.340
the intended manufacturing operation. The applicable checks

132
00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:35.240
depend on the equipment, process and approved

133
00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:35.700
procedures.

134
00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:39.490
Now imagine that a critical temperature sensor

135
00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:42.730
is found outside its calibration validity period.

136
00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:46.030
The appropriate response is not simply to

137
00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:46.830
continue production.

138
00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:50.120
The situation should be assessed through the

139
00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:53.280
relevant quality procedures to determine the equipment

140
00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.260
suitability for use and any potential product

141
00:07:56.260 --> 00:08:00.740
quality impact. This example demonstrates how GMP

142
00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:02.640
principles guide everyday decisions.

143
00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:05.280
Let us review the main points from

144
00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.360
this lesson. GMP supports the consistent manufacture

145
00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.960
and control of pharmaceutical products. Effective GMP

146
00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:17.240
depends on trained personnel, suitable facilities and

147
00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:21.760
equipment, controlled procedures, reliable documentation, and an

148
00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:22.940
effective quality system.

149
00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:26.440
Product quality cannot be assured by finished

150
00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:30.260
product testing alone. Every person involved in

151
00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:34.260
pharmaceutical operations contributes to maintaining quality and

152
00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:35.100
protecting patients.

153
00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:38.070
In our next lesson, we will explore

154
00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:41.490
the pharmaceutical quality system and good documentation

155
00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:44.790
practices in greater detail. Thank you for

156
00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.670
learning with ValiStera Academy.
